---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Tailscale"
  description="Discover your Tailscale machines in mxwl. Open remote workspaces, use your tailnet identity for SSH, and check on coding agents from your phone."
>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <div class="eyebrow">YOUR TAILNET / YOUR WORKSPACE</div>
    <h1>
      Your machines.
      <br />A little closer<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Leave the heavy lifting on your workstation. Bring its code, terminals, and agents into the
      editor on your laptop. Tailscale gets you there.
    </p>
    <div class="button-row">
      <a class="button primary" href="/docs/tailscale/">
        Connect your first machine <Icon name="remote" size={17} />
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/remote/">
        Explore remote work
      </a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <Screenshot
      src="/images/mxwl-tailscale.png"
      alt="Tailscale discovery in mxwl showing sample build, development, and studio machines with mesh addresses, SSH availability, tags, and online status."
      caption="Find a machine. Choose a username. Open a workspace."
      label="mxwl / Tailscale discovery · demo devices"
      eager
    />
  </div>
  <section class="container page-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">ALREADY ON YOUR NETWORK</div>
        <h2>
          A shorter path
          <br />
          to the work.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        mxwl reads the Tailscale connection on your computer. Your visible machines become reusable
        connections, ready to attach to any project.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-index">
      <div class="index-item">
        <div class="index-top">
          <Icon name="remote" size={26} />
          <span class="number">[01]</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Discover. Don’t transcribe.</h3>
        <p>
          Search names, DNS names, mesh addresses, and tags. See which peers are online and which
          advertise Tailscale SSH. No API token needed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="index-item">
        <div class="index-top">
          <Icon name="terminal" size={26} />
          <span class="number">[02]</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Bring your identity.</h3>
        <p>
          Tailscale SSH uses your tailnet identity. Your policy decides who can log in as which
          remote user. Ordinary SSH over the mesh works with your existing credentials too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="index-item">
        <div class="index-top">
          <Icon name="grid" size={26} />
          <span class="number">[03]</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Keep the same workspace.</h3>
        <p>
          Edit files over SFTP. Run terminals, services, and agents on the remote host. Review
          changes and use the embedded desktop browser in the same layout.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="statement">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">THE CODE STAYS THERE / THE CONTEXT STAYS HERE</div>
        <h2>
          Big workstation.
          <br />
          Small laptop.
          <br />
          Same flow.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Your remote machine owns the checkout and runs the tools. Your laptop holds the editor,
          the conversation, and the browser. Switch hosts and workspaces directly from the tree on the left.
        </p>
        <p>
          One machine connection can serve several projects. Each keeps its own checkout paths,
          settings, and browser identities.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/projects/">
          How projects and hosts fit together
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section">
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">A FEW MINUTES TO YOUR FIRST CONNECTION</div>
        <h2>
          Connect once.
          <br />
          Come back anytime.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Install and sign in to Tailscale on both machines. Enable Tailscale SSH on a supported
          remote host, or use its existing SSH server over the tailnet.
        </p>
        <p>
          In mxwl, open <strong>Projects → Add host → New machine connection → Tailscale</strong>.
          Pick the machine, confirm its remote username, and point the project at its checkout.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/tailscale/">
          The complete setup guide
        </a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <CodeBlock
          label="On the remote machine / enable Tailscale SSH"
          code="tailscale set --ssh"
        />
        <p class="small-copy">
          Tailscale SSH needs network and SSH access rules in your tailnet policy. Check mode may
          require browser approval before you connect.
        </p>
        <p class="small-copy">
          Using a normal SSH server? Choose <strong>Show all devices</strong> and configure your SSH
          agent, key, or password. Discovery shows availability; your credentials and policy grant
          access.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://tailscale.com/docs/features/tailscale-ssh">
          Tailscale SSH & supported platforms
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container page-section section-divider">
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">STEP AWAY / STAY IN THE LOOP</div>
        <h2>
          Your agents.
          <br />
          In your pocket.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Connect your phone to the tailnet and open mxwl’s mobile control dashboard. See what’s
          running, send a prompt, or answer a permission request while the desktop keeps working.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/tailscale/#check-on-agents-from-your-phone">
          Set up phone access
        </a>
      </div>
      <ul class="detail-list">
        <li>
          <Icon name="check" size={18} />
          <span>
            <strong>Opt in from Settings.</strong> Enable the control server and allow devices on
            your network.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Icon name="check" size={18} />
          <span>
            <strong>Use the desktop’s mesh address.</strong> Open its configured control port; the
            default is 9233.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Icon name="check" size={18} />
          <span>
            <strong>Keep the control token private.</strong> Tailnet access and the generated bearer
            token are both needed to control agents.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Icon name="check" size={18} />
          <span>
            <strong>No public port forwarding.</strong> Stay on your private network and keep mxwl
            running.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Closing />
</Base>
